# Backend API Architecture

## Stack
*   Node.js `"type": "module"` — import/export only.
*   Express 5, mounted at `/api` (development and test) and `/course-t1` (production).
*   Sequelize 6 + mysql2; config in `app/config/db.config.js` via dotenv.
*   Separate `sequelizeInstance.js`; models register in `models/index.js`.
*   bcryptjs + jsonwebtoken + Session model for auth.
*   winston + morgan (morgan streams to winston).

## server.js Responsibilities
*   `db.sequelize.sync()` on startup; `{ alter: true }` in development unless `SEQUELIZE_SYNC_ALTER=false`.
*   CORS with `credentials: true` and the frontend origin.
*   `express.json()` and `urlencoded({ extended: true })`.
*   Mount routes: `app.use(API_PREFIX, routes)`, then `app.use(errorHandler)` **last**.
*   Export `app` as default; call `listen()` only when `NODE_ENV !== "test"`.

## Route Layout
*   `app/routes/index.js` — central router registering each `*.routes.js`.
*   Auth at mount root: `POST /register`, `POST /login`, `POST /logout`.
*   Resource routes: `router.use("/courses", courseRoutes)`; one `resource.routes.js` per resource.
*   Multi-word URL segments are kebab-case (`/course-listing`).
*   Nested children on the parent router: `GET /sections/:sectionId/students`.

```javascript
router.get("/", [authenticate], courseController.findAll);
router.get("/:id", [authenticate], courseController.findOne);
router.post("/", [authenticateAdmin], courseController.create);
router.put("/:id", [authenticateAdmin], courseController.update);
router.delete("/:id", [authenticateAdmin], courseController.remove);
```

## Status Codes
| Code | When |
|------|------|
| `400` | Invalid input, invalid id, or a duplicate unique value |
| `401` | Not signed in — missing, invalid, or expired token (the frontend signs the user out) |
| `403` | Signed in but wrong role — `{ message: "Not Authorized." }` |
| `404` | Row not found, **or** not owned by the caller (never `403` for someone else's row) |
| `500` | Unexpected error — sent only by `errorHandler` |

## Controller Pattern
Controllers **throw** errors; they never `try/catch` just to send a 500. Express 5 forwards thrown errors and rejected promises to `errorHandler` (`app/helpers/errors.js`), which sends `{ message }` with the right status and logs anything unexpected.

```javascript
import db from "../models/index.js";
import { parseToNumber } from "../helpers/fields.js";
import { badRequest, notFound } from "../helpers/errors.js";

const exports = {};

exports.update = async (req, res) => {
  const courseId = parseToNumber(req.params.id);
  if (courseId === null) {
    throw badRequest("Invalid course id.");
  }

  const course = await db.course.findByPk(courseId);
  if (!course) {
    throw notFound("Course", courseId);
  }

  const { message, values } = validateCourse(req.body ?? {});
  if (message) {
    throw badRequest(message);
  }

  await course.update(values);
  return res.send(course);
};

export default exports;
```

*   Error helpers: `badRequest(message)`, `unauthorized(message)`, `forbidden()`, `notFound(label, id)`. Pass a capitalized label: `notFound("Course", id)`.
*   Parse route ids with `parseToNumber(req.params.id)`; throw `badRequest("Invalid <resource> id.")` on `null`. Read ids from `req.params` only, never from the body.
*   Validate the body with one `validate<Resource>(body)` function shared by `create` and `update`. It returns `{ message }` for the first problem (naming the field) or `{ values }` (trimmed, ready to save).
*   Save only `values` from the validator — never pass `req.body` straight to `create`/`update`.
*   Use `logger.debug/warn/error` — never `console.log`. Controllers don't log errors; `errorHandler` does.
*   Sequelize `include` for associations; always add `["id", "ASC"]` as the last `order` entry so paging is stable.

## Express 5 Gotchas
*   `req.body` is `undefined` when no body was parsed — use `req.body ?? {}`.
*   `req.query` is read-only — put parsed values elsewhere; never reassign it.
*   Repeated query keys become arrays (`?x=a&x=b`); bracket syntax (`?x[]=a`) is **not** parsed. List filters accept repeated keys or commas (`?department=Art,English`).
*   Route wildcards must be named (`/*splat`, not `*`).

## Uniqueness
Declare unique fields in the model with a message, and let the database enforce them. Do **not** look up duplicates before saving — `errorHandler` turns `UniqueConstraintError` into `400 { message }`.

```javascript
number: {
  type: Sequelize.STRING,
  allowNull: false,
  unique: { msg: "Course number is already taken." },
},
```

For a rule across several columns, use a model-level unique index and cover the duplicate case in tests.

## JSON Responses
*   **Success:** `res.send(data)`, `res.status(201).send(created)` for creates.
*   **Failure:** `{ message: "Human-readable explanation." }` — via a thrown error helper, not `res.status(4xx)` in controllers.
*   **404 format:** ``{ message: `Course with id=${id} not found.` }`` (produced by `notFound`).
*   **Paginated lists:** `{ items, total, page, pageSize, pageCount }`. Defaults: `page=1`, `pageSize=20`, maximum `100`.
*   Do **not** use `{ success, data }` envelopes.

## Model Conventions
*   Sensitive fields excluded via `defaultScope` (e.g. User password).
*   Use `Model.unscoped()` only when password compare is required at login.
*   Associations (`hasMany`/`belongsTo`) defined in `models/index.js`, not in controllers.

## Schema Changes
The schema is created from the models by `sequelize.sync()`. There are no migration scripts. After changing unique indexes, check `SHOW INDEX FROM <table>;` — `alter` sync can add duplicate indexes.

## Protected Routes
*   `authenticate` reads `Authorization: Bearer <token>`, finds a non-expired Session row, and sets `req.user = { id, role }`. Missing or invalid → `401`.
*   `authenticateAdmin` does the same, then returns `403 { message: "Not Authorized." }` for non-admins.
*   Row ownership checks belong in `app/authorization/` helpers, not inline in controllers (see `security.mdc`).
